# The Glory of Khadi

Black money accumulated with the wealthy sends prices ever upward while the poor writhe like worms. Public anger erupts, so Nanda orders the police to destroy gambling fortresses and seize illegal money. An officer sets out declaring his duty and authority to search everywhere.

Six-fingered Hariram Seth hears the command without fear. He boasts that he helped overthrow arrogant British rule, broke laws, lived at Sabarmati, conducted satyagrahas, and built Congress. He may do business, but must not be mistaken for a mere businessman: he is national fire, and no Nanda would defy him.

Hariram explains that elections require him to labour. Politicians tremble while speaking of black markets, profiteering, and sacks of black money, yet stretch out their hands at election time. Great sums must be accumulated to give them; slaughtering the goose would end its eggs.

The khadi-clad officer briefly worries but searches anyway, finding accounts and gold. The public praises his efficiency. Hariram's agents pressure ministers: could they antagonize a man who has already given half a crore, funds lavish ministerial feasts, supplies thousands in donations, and provides the strength to subdue opposition?

Hariram sends telegrams accusing the officer of injustice and threatens political relatives and ministerial fortunes. While the officer happily reads press praise, a transfer order arrives. Headquarters declares that he confused the accounts of “Nara Hariram” with Hariram Seth's. The difference, Hariram jokes, is only two letters and four thousand rupees. Nara Hari is merely his poor employee with fourteen children and a mansion rented for thirty rupees; he gave him money out of pity. Who can contradict him?

After escaping trouble, Hariram presides when a minister opens a Vande Mataram hall. The minister praises him; a new officer circulates showing kindness to the khadi wearer and says Hariram will work from now for electoral victory. Hariram rises and vows body, soul, and wealth to Mother India, then asks that British-era official practices be completely abolished, while admitting irregularities still occur.

The minister says injustice must always be reported. A new committee to prevent official injustice is immediately formed - with Hariram as chairman. Hariram promises to remove any difficulty officials cause if people report it to him.

Reading this in the newspapers, the transferred officer sighs: such indeed is the glory hidden inside khadi.

---NOTES---
The title is wholly ironic. Khadi, once a sign of anti-colonial sacrifice, becomes the protective costume of a wealthy political financier who defeats investigation and is appointed to police injustice.

